Gateway connection-test construction failures
============================================

Connection-test adapter construction now belongs to the same fixed error
boundary as test execution. A constructor rejection produces a failed test
result with a safe message and measured latency instead of an unhandled HTTP
error. Recognized authentication, connectivity and timeout categories retain
the existing guidance. Other exceptions use the existing generic message.
Raw exception text and tracebacks are not emitted by this service boundary.

This includes saved HTTP selections that contradict required certificate
verification. The actual MikroTik and pfSense constructors still reject that
policy before opening an HTTP client. Direct adapter construction continues
to raise its original exception. The service does not silently change protocol,
disable verification or rewrite saved settings to make a test pass.

Only a successfully acquired adapter is disconnected. Caller cancellation
continues to propagate; cleanup of an acquired adapter is unchanged. Resources
allocated internally by a constructor that then raises remain that constructor's
responsibility because no adapter object was returned to this service.

Qualification covers saved and unsaved HTTP routes backed by PostgreSQL, with
synthetic authenticated identities and constructor error injections across four
vendors. Actual encrypted saved rows and real MikroTik/pfSense constructors
reproduce the HTTP 500 before the fix and the controlled failure afterward,
while creating no transport client. Real HTTP/TLS peers additionally protect
the saved-protocol behavior as regression controls. These transport fixtures
deliberately disable certificate verification; they do not prove certificate
trust or hardware compatibility. Existing direct-adapter policy refusal tests
remain intact; service refusal assertions now verify the controlled contract.

Saved-test resource authority and stale-result persistence are covered by
GATEWAY-TEST-AUTHORITY. Current caller/session/grant revalidation, arbitrary
provider metadata and other operation logs remain separate. No schema, dependency
or hardware changes are made. See GATEWAY-SAVED-TEST-POLICY and
GATEWAY-TEST-DIAGNOSTICS for the related boundaries.
